  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1048 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nUnsettled weather returns to the region Wednesday as a compact\nshortwave crosses the area. The precipitation will generally\narrive in three waves. The initial round will arrive early\nWednesday as weak warm air advection ahead of the shortwave leads\nto a region of frontogenesis through the mid to late morning \nhours, resulting in an west-east oriented broad area of light \nprecipitation with around 40-60% chance at any one location across\ncentral to east-central Wisconsin. A break is then possible for \nmost of the area before additional showers develop along a cold \nfront that arrives along with the upper shortwave. These showers \nwill be more cellular, resulting in on and off light rain showers\nfor much of the region. Would expect the best rain chances to be\nassociated with the passage of the cold front in the afternoon \nand evening. Finally, the third round will arrive as cold air \nadvection behind the front drives lake effect showers through the \novernight hours and through much of a Thursday. Despite the \npresence of rain in the forecast throughout the period, the \noverall rain potential will still be relatively light. A \nwidespread tenth of an inch of total rainfall is expected for most\n(50-70% chance), but probability of exceeding a quarter of an \ninch is limited to the counties along the Upper Peninsula and \nnorthern Door County. Rain chances then drop to near zero \novernight everywhere but the typical lake effect regions of far \nnorth- central Wisconsin and portions of Lake Michigan shoreline \nbut especially northern Door County. The continued potential for \nlake effect showers will increase rain potential for these areas, \nwith around 30-40% chance of exceeding half an inch of rainfall \nWednesday through Thursday for north-central Vilas and northern \nDoor County. Finally, some snow may still mix into the lake effect\nshowers at times Wednesday night and again Thursday night. While \nmost will likely not see any of this snow accumulate, the \npersistent signal for some lake effect snow showers has kept the \nprobability of around a half an inch for northern Vilas at around \n40-60% chance. Door County may also see a few flurries, but \naccumulations there will likely be inhibited by warm ground \nsurfaces and daytime highs.\n\nTemperatures will see a return to near or slightly below normal\nwith the influx of cooler air, with highs in the upper 30s to 40s\nThursday and Friday before gradually trending above normal over\nthe weekend.",
